import json

with open('/app/output/analysis_results.json', 'r') as f:
    data = json.load(f)
with open('/app/output/charts_manifest.json', 'r') as f:
    charts = json.load(f)

kpis = data['kpis']

html = f'''<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit — 2026 Catalog Analysis</title>
<style>
  :root {{
    --primary: #1f4e79;
    --accent: #c55a11;
    --text: #222222;
    --muted: #555555;
    --bg: #f7f8fa;
    --card: #ffffff;
    --border: #e2e4e8;
  }}
  * {{ box-sizing: border-box; }}
  body {{
    margin: 0;
    font-family: "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    color: var(--text);
    background: var(--bg);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }}
  header {{
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--primary) 0%, #14314d 100%);
    color: #fff;
    padding: 40px 20px;
    text-align: center;
  }}
  header h1 {{ margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.9rem; font-weight: 700; }}
  header p {{ margin: 0; opacity: 0.9; font-size: 1.05rem; }}
  .container {{
    max-width: 1100px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 30px 20px;
  }}
  .kpi-grid {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(170px, 1fr));
    gap: 18px;
    margin: 28px 0 36px;
  }}
  .kpi-card {{
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 22px 18px;
    text-align: center;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.04);
  }}
  .kpi-value {{
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--primary);
    line-height: 1.2;
  }}
  .kpi-label {{
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 6px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.4px;
  }}
  section {{
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 28px 30px;
    margin-bottom: 24px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.04);
  }}
  h2 {{
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    color: var(--primary);
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 10px;
  }}
  h3 {{
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--primary);
    margin: 22px 0 10px;
  }}
  p {{ margin: 0 0 12px; }}
  ul {{ margin: 0 0 12px; padding-left: 22px; }}
  li {{ margin-bottom: 6px; }}
  .chart {{ margin: 18px 0; text-align: center; }}
  .chart img {{ max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 6px; }}
  .highlight {{
    background: #fff7ed;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    margin: 14px 0;
  }}
  table {{
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 14px 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }}
  th, td {{ text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }}
  th {{ background: #f0f3f7; color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600; }}
  tr:hover {{ background: #fafbfd; }}
  .note {{ font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }}
  footer {{
    text-align: center;
    padding: 24px 20px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<header>
  <h1>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit — 2026 Catalog Analysis</h1>
  <p>A data-driven analytical report on the 14,875 active satellites currently in orbit</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

  <!-- KPI Section -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['total_satellites']:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Active Satellites</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['leo_share_pct']}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">LEO Share</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['starlink_count']:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Starlink Constellation</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['median_inclination']}°</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Median Inclination</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{kpis['since_2020']:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Launched Since 2020</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section: Overview -->
  <section id="overview">
    <h2>Catalog Overview & Key Figures</h2>
    <p>This report analyzes the full catalog of <strong>{kpis['total_satellites']:,} active satellites</strong> sourced from the CelesTrak space-tracking platform (via NORAD TLE data). The dataset spans launch years from 1964 to 2026, with orbital element epochs ranging from <strong>{data['epoch_range']['min'][:10]}</strong> to <strong>{data['epoch_range']['max'][:10]}</strong>, confirming high data freshness.</p>
    <p>Key orbital parameters show a highly concentrated population:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mean Motion:</strong> The vast majority of satellites orbit at ≥15 rev/day (n = {data['mm_dist'].get('≥15', 0):,}), consistent with large LEO constellations. Only {data['mm_dist'].get('<1', 0):,} objects have mean motion below 1 rev/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Inclination:</strong> The median inclination is <strong>{kpis['median_inclination']}°</strong>, driven by Starlink shells near 53° and polar orbits near 90°. The distribution peaks in the 50–70° band ({data['inc_dist'].get('50–70°', 0):,} satellites) and the 85–100° polar band ({data['inc_dist'].get('85–100°', 0):,} satellites).</li>
      <li><strong>Eccentricity:</strong> {data['ecc_dist'].get('<0.01', 0):,} satellites ({round(data['ecc_dist'].get('<0.01',0)/kpis['total_satellites']*100,1)}%) have near-circular orbits (eccentricity &lt;0.01). Only {data['ecc_dist'].get('>0.5', 0):,} objects exhibit highly eccentric trajectories (&gt;0.5).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Orbit Regime -->
  <section id="orbit-regime">
    <h2>Orbital Regime Classification</h2>
    <p>Satellites were classified using <strong>MEAN_MOTION thresholds</strong> applied to the catalog data:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>LEO (Low Earth Orbit):</strong> &gt;14 rev/day (~160–2,000 km altitude)</li>
      <li><strong>MEO (Medium Earth Orbit):</strong> 1.5–14 rev/day</li>
      <li><strong>GEO (Geostationary Orbit):</strong> 0.9–1.5 rev/day</li>
      <li><strong>HEO (Highly Elliptical Orbit):</strong> Eccentricity &gt;0.25 regardless of mean motion</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['orbit_regime']}" alt="Orbital Regime Distribution"></div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Regime</th><th>Count</th><th>Share</th></tr>
'''

for reg in ['LEO', 'MEO', 'GEO', 'HEO']:
    cnt = data['orbit_counts'].get(reg, 0)
    share = round(cnt / kpis['total_satellites'] * 100, 1)
    html += f'      <tr><td>{reg}</td><td>{cnt:,}</td><td>{share}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''    </table>
    <div class="highlight">
      <strong>Key insight:</strong> LEO dominates the catalog with {data['orbit_counts'].get('LEO',0):,} satellites ({round(data['orbit_counts'].get('LEO',0)/kpis['total_satellites']*100,1)}% of the total). This concentration is almost entirely due to mega-constellations — Starlink alone accounts for {kpis['starlink_count']:,} LEO spacecraft, representing {round(kpis['starlink_count']/data['orbit_counts'].get('LEO',0)*100,1)}% of all LEO satellites.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Constellation Analysis -->
  <section id="constellation-analysis">
    <h2>Major Constellation & Operator Breakdown</h2>
    <p>Constellation membership was inferred by parsing <code>OBJECT_NAME</code> prefixes. The resulting operator landscape is heavily skewed toward a single provider:</p>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['constellations']}" alt="Top Constellations"></div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Operator / Constellation</th><th>Active Satellites</th><th>Share of Total</th></tr>
'''

for const, cnt in list(data['const_counts'].items())[:10]:
    share = round(cnt / kpis['total_satellites'] * 100, 1)
    html += f'      <tr><td>{const}</td><td>{cnt:,}</td><td>{share}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''    </table>
    <div class="highlight">
      <strong>Starlink dominance:</strong> SpaceX’s Starlink constellation comprises <strong>{kpis['starlink_count']:,} active satellites</strong> — {round(kpis['starlink_count']/kpis['total_satellites']*100,1)}% of the entire catalog. No other operator exceeds 1,000 spacecraft; the next largest identifiable group is OneWeb ({data['const_counts'].get('OneWeb',0):,}). The remaining {data['const_counts'].get('Other',0):,} satellites span a fragmented mix of government, scientific, and commercial missions.
    </div>
    <h3>Orbital Regime: Starlink vs. All Others</h3>
    <p>Starlink operates exclusively in LEO, whereas the non-Starlink population retains a more traditional mix of LEO, MEO, and GEO assets:</p>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['starlink_vs_others']}" alt="Starlink vs Others by Regime"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Launch Trends -->
  <section id="launch-trends">
    <h2>Launch Year Trends</h2>
    <p>Launch year was extracted from the first four characters of <code>OBJECT_ID</code>. The resulting time series reveals a dramatic acceleration in the active satellite population, especially after 2019.</p>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['launch_trends']}" alt="Launch Year Trends"></div>
    <p>Of the {kpis['total_satellites']:,} currently active satellites, <strong>{kpis['since_2020']:,} ({round(kpis['since_2020']/kpis['total_satellites']*100,1)}%)</strong> were launched in 2020 or later. This reflects the rapid deployment of Starlink and competing broadband constellations. Prior to 2019, annual additions to the active fleet rarely exceeded a few hundred spacecraft; since 2020, yearly launch counts have risen into the thousands.</p>
    <div class="highlight">
      <strong>Headline:</strong> The post-2020 launch surge has added more active satellites in six years than the previous five decades combined. For spectrum regulators and debris-mitigation planners, this is the defining trend of the current orbital environment.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Orbital Characteristics -->
  <section id="orbital-characteristics">
    <h2>Orbital Parameter Deep Dive</h2>
    <h3>Inclination</h3>
    <p>Inclination determines a satellite’s ground-track coverage and launch-site latitude requirements. The catalog shows two dominant clusters:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>~53° inclination:</strong> The Starlink shell population drives the 50–70° band ({data['inc_dist'].get('50–70°',0):,} satellites).</li>
      <li><strong>Near-polar (~90°):</strong> Earth-observation and some communications missions populate the 85–100° band ({data['inc_dist'].get('85–100°',0):,} satellites).</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['inclination_dist']}" alt="Inclination Distribution"></div>
    <p>Starlink’s own inclination statistics: mean = {round(data['starlink_inc_stats']['mean'],2)}°, median = {round(data['starlink_inc_stats']['50%'],2)}°, std = {round(data['starlink_inc_stats']['std'],2)}°. The tight interquartile range (25th–75th percentile: {round(data['starlink_inc_stats']['25%'],2)}°–{round(data['starlink_inc_stats']['75%'],2)}°) confirms disciplined shell deployment.</p>

    <h3>Eccentricity</h3>
    <p>Almost the entire active fleet ({data['ecc_dist'].get('<0.01',0):,} of {kpis['total_satellites']:,}) maintains near-circular orbits (eccentricity &lt;0.01). High-eccentricity outliers ({data['ecc_dist'].get('>0.5',0):,} satellites) are predominantly legacy scientific or communications satellites in Molniya-like orbits.</p>
    <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['eccentricity_dist']}" alt="Eccentricity Distribution"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Conclusions -->
  <section id="conclusions">
    <h2>Key Takeaways & Outlook</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Orbital congestion is now a LEO story.</strong> With {round(data['orbit_counts'].get('LEO',0)/kpis['total_satellites']*100,1)}% of active satellites in Low Earth Orbit, spectrum coordination and conjunction-assessment workloads are concentrated in altitudes below 2,000 km.</li>
      <li><strong>One operator reshapes global totals.</strong> Starlink’s {kpis['starlink_count']:,} satellites represent {round(kpis['starlink_count']/kpis['total_satellites']*100,1)}% of the catalog. Any policy framework for space sustainability must account for the statistical dominance of a single constellation.</li>
      <li><strong>Growth is historically unprecedented.</strong> {kpis['since_2020']:,} satellites — {round(kpis['since_2020']/kpis['total_satellites']*100,1)}% of the active fleet — were launched in the last six years. Licensing regimes and debris-mitigation guidelines designed for slower eras will face pressure to adapt.</li>
      <li><strong>Most orbits are predictable.</strong> Near-circular paths and tightly clustered inclinations simplify tracking and collision-risk modeling, but the sheer volume of objects raises the aggregate probability of close approaches.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">All figures derived directly from the CelesTrak active-satellite catalog (data.csv). Orbital regime classifications use MEAN_MOTION thresholds as stated above; constellation assignments rely on OBJECT_NAME prefix matching.</p>
  </section>

</div>

<footer>
  Generated from CelesTrak NORAD TLE data · {kpis['total_satellites']:,} active satellites · Analysis date 2026-04-29
</footer>

</body>
</html>
'''

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print("Report written to /app/output/final_report.html")
